The Silence of the Lambs is undoubtedly  one of the most iconic horror films of  all time  but even diehard fans might not know  about the real-world research onset  pranking and bizarre costume choices  that went into its production here is  the untold truth of Hannibal Lecter's  finest hour  although Dino De Laurentiis bought the  rights to the name Hannibal Lecter when  his studio made 1986 his Manhunter the  very nature of the Beast kept other  filmmakers away for years it makes sense  after all a cannibal psychiatrist is a  tough sell for a movie that is until  Gene Hackman and Arthur Krim of Orion  Pictures teamed up to buy the rights  Hackman was originally interested in  directing and wanted to play Lecter at  the same time but he later dropped out  after his daughter read the book and  objected to the story's heavy violence  créme bought Hackman's half of the  rights and Jonathan Demi intrigued by  the unusual story signed on its director  Demi initially considered a number of  actresses to play Clarice Starling  including Michelle Pfeiffer meg Ryan and  Laura Dern but Jodie Foster who had just  won an Oscar for the accused lobbied  hard for the parts her grit talent and  intelligence shone through and  ultimately landed her the role for  Hannibal Lecter almost every single  prestige actor of the day was on the  table Sean Connery Robert De Niro and  Dustin Hoffman were all under  consideration by now of course it's  impossible to picture anyone but Hopkins  wearing that infamous Muslim when you  think of The Silence of the Lambs one of  the first images that likely pops to  mind is dr. Lecter in his iconic Mass  strapped to an upright stretcher in the  movie itself  he has been restrained to meet Catherine  Martin's mother so he can pass on the  information he's been withholding about  Catherine's kidnapper the grill over the  mouth allows him to speak and be heard  but not bite as he's often fond of doing  of course this provides the opportunity  for some striking imagery to The  Criterion Collection has released some  terrifying behind-the-scenes footage of  Hopkins trying on a number of different  masks before settling on the one that  appears on screen one example is modeled  after a baseball umpire mask another is  a full night's helmet that makes Hector  look more like a Monty Python character  than one of the most chilling killers of  yet another makes him look like a  beekeeper luckily the choice that ended  up on screen is perfectly horrible and  gives the audience the chance to stare a  little longer intellect errs hypnotic  peepers the majority of the Silence of  the Lambs is seen from Clarice  starlings perspective one of the most  haunting moments of the film however  happens through a very different pair of  eyes Katherine Martin the girl kidnapped  by Buffalo Bill is trapped in a well in  his home  up above Buffalo Bill himself applies  makeup and dances around his dressing  room in pretty much the creepiest way  you can possibly imagine  but this dance wasn't actually scripted  nor were some of bills adornments like  the tattoo on his chest screenwriter Ted  tally got quite a shock when he finally  saw these moments unfold on screen  Buffalo Bill actor Ted Levine who had  done copious research to develop the  character was so nervous to film this  scene that he actually took shots of  tequila to get through it but it all  paid off  producer Edward saxons said about  Levine's performance the scene took a  lot of courage it wasn't in the script  and for me it's a very moving scene it  identifies the pain and twisted  psychology of this character as anyone  who's seen this movie will know Buffalo  Bill is a doozy of a serial killer he  kidnaps women starves them and  eventually kills them all in an effort  to create a woman suit out of their skin  and it was the real-life Wisconsin  serial killer  Ed Gein upon whom psycho is also based  who served as the inspiration for  Buffalo Bills particular obsessions Ted  Bundy's infamous methods were  incorporated into Bill's kidnapping  technique - he - were a fake cast to  feign disability then used said cast to  knock his victims out the third source  of dark inspiration for Buffalo Bill was  Gary Michael - a Philadelphia nurse who  had a penchant for kidnapping women and  shackling them in his basement these  three real killers alongside a few  others contribute to the terrifying  composite that is Buffalo Bill but he's  not the only character rooted in real  world figures Jack Crawford is based on  real-life criminal profiler John Douglas  who is also the inspiration behind the  Netflix series mine hunter Jodie Foster  also worked with FBI agent Mary Ann  Krause  who provided invaluable insight into  Clarisse starlings circumstances the  moment when Clarisse cries in the  privacy of her car after her first  encounter with dr. Lecter rang  particularly true for Kraus who says I  saw it and thought now that's like me  The Silence of the Lambs commands a  great deal of respect among movie fans  but it has also come under fire over the  years for its presentation of members of  the LGBTQ community  critics decry buffalo bill as a twisted  caricature of gay and transgender people  capable of doing real damage to a  vulnerable population caterpillar into  chrysalis or pupa beauty all Betty wants  to change Jonathan Demi has addressed  these criticisms saying Buffalo Bill  wasn't a gay character he was a  tormented man who hated himself and  wished he was a woman because that would  have made him as far away from himself  as he possibly could be unfortunately  this doesn't come across as clearly as  demi would have liked in the film  especially considering some of the  problematic language the movie uses to  talk about transgender people Ted Levine  agrees with Demi however saying here's a  guy who imagines himself with this kind  of feminine power you know this  spiritual kind of mother power serial  killers all pursue that feminine energy  that female persona they get both  masculinity and femininity wrapped into  someone and that's like perfection real  power and that's what gum is after the  Silence of the Lambs is pretty much as  dark and suspenseful as a film can get  so you can imagine that filming it might  not be a barrel of laughs  to keep spirits up in the midst of such  intensity games and pranks became the  norm on set as Brooke Smith who portrays  kidnapped Katherine Martin told People  magazine everything was a joke even  though she and Hopkins had no scenes  together he would often come up to her  in character as the fearsome Hannibal  Lecter and give her flowers and  chocolates the crew also made a board  game based on a blueprint of the set  that they called thumbs game the aim of  which was to save Katherine Jonathan  Demi liked to play pranks on his actors  including leading lady Jodie Foster one  memorable gag took place as Foster  filmed a now-deleted scene of Claire  picking up a rental car Dimmie had the  rental car agent really test Clarisse  about who she was before giving her the  keys demanding ID foster could not of  course provide being the consummate  professional she is foster didn't break  character until she realized what was  going on the movie poster for The  Silence of the Lambs prominently  features Clarice Starling space with her  mouth covered by the death's-head moth  that buffalo bill likes to insert into  his victims throats but only the most  eagle-eyed fans might have noticed the  skull on the moth isn't actually a skull  at all the image is lifted from  surrealist painter Salvador Dali's 1951  photo envelope test mores enveloped us  Moors or voluptuous death portrays a  human skull formed out of naked women's  bodies dolly posed to the left of the  women wearing a top hat and an intense  expression rounds out the piece  obviously for the Silence of the Lambs  poster Dolly was removed only the women  remained in an artful nod to Buffalo  Bills modus operandi while there isn't a  whole lot of Dolly's characteristic  surrealism in the Silence of the Lambs  in many ways Buffalo Bills project is a  horrible exercise and absurdity of dolly  s proportions you might think that  releasing a best-selling novel might  make any author happy especially when  it's turned into a widely beloved film  funnily enough though Silence of the  Lambs offered Thomas Harris has never  and will never watch the movie or any of  the many other adaptations of his work  that have graced the big and small  screens  Jonathan Demi has said this is because  Harris heard an anecdote about author  John Locke or a seeing an episode of the  BBC's adaptation of Smiley's people his  classic spy novel which featured Sir  Alec Guinness as his leading man  the Caray was so astounded by Genesis  performance that he came to consider  smiley as belonging to the actor instead  of him harris feared that seeing Hopkins  as Lecter would produce the same effect  loosening the character from his grasp  as an author this is despite the fact  that the Lecter of the books is  Lithuanian and has red eyes and a sixth  finger on his left hand but that's the  power of Anthony Hopkins for you like  his colleagues Scott Glenn did a great  deal of research when working on his  character FBI agent Jack Crawford  he spent a lot of time with John Douglas  the profiler on whom his character had  been based and Douglas didn't pull any  punches and showing Glenn the kinds of  things he'd seen in his long career as  Glenn told People magazine  I lost a certain degree of innocence to  this day I find myself having unpleasant  dreams about the things I found out in  fact Glenn's time at the FBI training  facility in Quantico was so profoundly  upsetting that he changed his political  stance from being against capital  punishment and now broadly agrees with  it as Glenn himself says the experience  in Quantico changed my mind about that  for all time a disgraced Clarice  Starling makes her second appearance in  the 2001 The Silence of the Lambs  follow-up Hannibal but Jodie Foster  elected not to return for the role and  Julianne Moore was cast instead this was  a smart move on Foster's part as  Hannibal ended up being one of the  weakest links in the Hannibal Lecter  franchise however in early 2020 CBS  announced its series commitment to a  dedicated Clarice Starling show entitled  Clarice that will take place one year  after the events of the Silence of the  Lambs Clarice is said to be a deep dive  into starlings career as a profiler of  serial killers as she simultaneously  navigates the sexism of the FBI but this  news shocked fans Brian Fuller's  arthouse horror series Hannibal which  was abruptly canceled after just three  seasons hopefully Clarice will satisfy  fans of the franchise with a searing  look into the life of The Silence of the  Lambs unforgettable heroine check out  one of our newest videos right here plus  even more looper videos about your  favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  you  [Music]

